pt09 adversarial lane, surface: clause splitting. Greens the RED frozen in the
previous commit.

`clauseSegments` already carried the principle: a hyphen ends a clause only
when it is not joining two word characters. It applied that to the hyphen
alone. Now a PERIOD joining two word characters is part of what it joins
("3.5", "u.s", "e.g"), a COMMA between two digits is part of the number
("1,000"), and a period closing a known abbreviation is part of the
abbreviation — that last case being the one the joining rule cannot see,
because a space follows it.

The abbreviation set is the same one the sentence merger already keeps
(MERGE_ALWAYS_ABBREVIATION and MERGE_CONTINUATION_ABBREVIATION), case-folded
because this splitter reads normalizeText output. A bare "no." is deliberately
excluded: at clause granularity it cannot be told from the word "no" ending a
clause.

Why it mattered: every clause-scoped branch counts forward from the assertion
clause, so each of these pushed the claim's real next clause to +2 where
nothing reads it. One claim, one verdict, one difference:

  "…in most markets; that model is wrong."        Challenges 0.697
  "…in the U.S. market; that model is wrong."     Resembles  0.697

Identical score, opposite label, one abbreviation between them.
`clauseSegments` is shared by contextual co-fire and stance on purpose, so
this is one fix for both.

Cost, measured: gate floors identical (191 · 1.0000 · 1.0000 · 0.8529) and 0
of 1,299 corpus rows move — no score and no label. The zero is attributed, not
assumed: of the corpus's 782 matched claim units, 66 contain a decimal, 5 a
thousands comma and 23 an abbreviation period. The splitter reaches all 94 and
changes none of their outcomes, because in each the extra clause boundary fell
somewhere no clause-scoped branch was counting.

All 18 suite steps green.

